A||||2||ferg||Need help with struts and rear brakes||||1||
Z||000000||ferg||06-21-2001||12:19 AM||||I'm replacing the struts on my car and the chilton manual says to torque to 65 ft/lbs.<P>Do I let out the spring compression and then torque?<P>I was also replacing my brake pads, and I tried to compress the piston- but something happened and the piston cam flying out.<P>Now I have a the following things sitting on the floor-<P>piston<BR>3 washers- 2 regular - 1 with 3 ball-bearings<BR>spring<BR>gear<P>How do the washers get put together?<P>Does it go- washer-bearing washer-washer?<P>and to rebuild the caliper I do the following - put gear on the shaft inside the caliper, spring, washers, piston. <P>Is this correct?<P>Thanks<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||06-21-2001||04:10 PM||||My suggestion would be to get rebuilt calipers for your car disc brake calipers with the e brakes built into them are not the easiest things to rebuild if you have never done them before. I know I never have.And if you do get rebuilds I also suggest get them pre loaded with the brake pads already installed.not sure about your sruts,but I am sure there will be someone here to give you answer.good luck||||reg||3
Z||000002||Tigger||06-21-2001||04:36 PM||||To remove the strut, jack up the car from the center of the engine cradle, that way you can remove both sides at the same time.  Make sure to block the front wheels etc.  Jack until the rear wheels are off the ground, then remove the wheels and then loosen the two lower nuts/bolts on the strut to knuckle (they can be a pain, be careful that the arm of the wrench doesn't slip off and whack the wheelwell opening).  No need to worry about the potential energy from the spring because the spring is captured in the strut.  There is no more travel once the wheels are off the ground so it's not going anywhere.  Now, remove the three nuts from above.  however DO NOT remove the center nut at the top, doing so with the spring uncompressed is a potential energy disaster.<P>Take the strut/coil (modules) to a shop and have them exchange the struts using a compressor and air gun, only takes about 5 min job per strut (if that, and safer than fumbling with compressors) so they shouldn't charge you much.  Make sure they clock the lower part of the strut so it's in the same direction as it was before, they should be aware, but check for it.<P>Bolt them back in, the two lower bolts I think are 140 ft/lb (maxed out my torque wrench) and the upper three are much lower, 25 ft/lb.<P>Lastly get an alignment done.  You can mark the position of everything before you tear it apart, but it's better to make sure everything is square.<P>Hope this helps.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
